**1. Analysis of the Attached PDF:**

The research investigates Nano-11, a dendrimer-like α-D-glucan nanoparticle, as a vaccine adjuvant. Nano-11, with a positive surface charge and 70-80 nm diameter, efficiently adsorbs and delivers protein antigens to dendritic cells (DCs). It activates DCs, inducing costimulatory molecule expression and IL-1β secretion. In vivo, Nano-11 enhances antibody responses comparable to or exceeding aluminum adjuvants, while causing only transient, localized inflammation. It targets antigens to migratory DCs in draining lymph nodes without systemic distribution, highlighting its potential as a safe and effective next-generation adjuvant.

**2. Independent Analysis of the Image:**

The image is a Western blot displaying protein bands from cell supernatants and lysates, assessed against molecular weight markers (30kD, 20kD, 15kD). In the "Supernatant" lanes, a distinct band appears just below 15kD when cells are treated with Nano-11 (LPS-) and strongly with Nano-11 plus LPS (LPS+). In the "Cell lysate" lanes, a prominent band is observed around 30kD only when cells are primed with LPS (LPS+), irrespective of Nano-11 presence.

**3. Relevance of the Image to the PDF:**

The image is highly relevant to the research matter of the PDF. It is Fig. 7D from the 2015 article titled "Dendrimer-like alpha-D-glucan nanoparticles activate dendritic cells and are effective vaccine adjuvants," directly supporting the claim that Nano-11 induces IL-1β secretion. The Western blot visually confirms that Nano-11 promotes the cleavage of the inactive 30kD pro-IL-1β (found in cell lysates upon LPS priming) into its active 17kD form (secreted into the supernatant). This demonstrates a key mechanism by which Nano-11 activates dendritic cells as a vaccine adjuvant.